    {
      "subpart": "c",
      "stem": "$f(t) = \\frac{t + 9}{e^t}$",
      "final_answer": "$\\frac{-t - 8}{e^t}$",
      "mark_breakdown": [
        {
          "code": "M1",
          "desc": "Applying quotient rule $\\frac{u'v - uv'}{v^2}$"
        },
        {
          "code": "M1",
          "desc": "Cancelling common factor of $e^t$"
        },
        {
          "code": "A1",
          "desc": "Correct simplified derivative"
        }
      ],
      "solution": "$f(t) = \\frac{t + 9}{e^t}$\n$\\therefore f'(t) = \\frac{(1)e^t - e^t(t + 9)}{(e^t)^2} \\quad \\{\\text{quotient rule}\\}\n= \\frac{1 - (t + 9)}{e^t}\n= \\frac{-t - 8}{e^t}$",
      "ai_explanation": {
        "concept_rule": "Quotient rule followed by factorisation and cancellation of common exponential terms.",
        "step_by_step": [
          "Let $u = t + 9$ and $v = e^t$, so $u' = 1$ and $v' = e^t$.",
          "Apply quotient rule: $\\frac{1 \\cdot e^t - (t + 9)e^t}{(e^t)^2}$.",
          "Factor out $e^t$ in numerator: $\\frac{e^t[1 - (t + 9)]}{(e^t)^2}$.",
          "Cancel $e^t$: $\\frac{1 - t - 9}{e^t} = \\frac{-t - 8}{e^t}$."
        ],
        "common_pitfalls": "Forgetting brackets around $(t + 9)$ when subtracting: writing $1 - t + 9$ instead of $1 - t - 9$."
